Environment assumptions the helper test corpus makes.

Written after step 9, because three of the four CI failures during steps 5 to 7
were Linux-only behaviour that is invisible on macOS, and each one cost a red
build to rediscover. If you are reading this because of a failing test, the
cause is probably below.

------------------------------------------------------------------- the corpora

  t/test_policy.c        validators, the phase-one decoder, Model B    (step 4)
  t/test_state.c         paths, directories, locks, process identity   (step 5)
  t/test_registry.c      approval records                              (step 6)
  t/test_exec.c          the phase-two argv, element by element        (step 7)
  t/test_adversarial.c   attacks on all of the above                   (step 9)
  t/fixtures/auth/       fixtures shared with tests/twophase.bats      (step 9)
  t/test_closure.c       the trusted execution closure                 (step 10)
  t/test_integration.c   a real execve into a real second process       (step 11)
  t/integration/         the stand-in OpenConnect, and two scripts      (step 11)

The first four are organised by module and mostly assert that each piece does
what it says. The fifth is organised by ATTACK and mostly asserts refusals; it
is also where a test that failed against the implementation and produced a fix
is recorded, next to the fix.

Everything runs UNPRIVILEGED. That is a design property, not a limitation: the
state root, the registry root and the expected owner are all parameters, so the
code under test is byte-for-byte the code that runs as root. Nothing is a
weakened copy.

--------------------------------------------------------- fixtures live in $HOME

Not /tmp. On both macOS and Linux /tmp is mode 1777, and vu_path_trusted
correctly refuses any chain with a world-writable component — so a fixture there
makes the trusted-path tests fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the
code. The sticky bit does not help: the check is about write access to the
directory, and 1777 grants it.

Fixtures therefore go under $HOME with a pid in the name, and are removed with
vu_rm_rf() (t/harness.c), which is a shell-free depth-first unlinkat walk. It is
shell-free on purpose: building a shell command line out of a path is the
pattern this project's design document spends pages arguing against.

t/test_adversarial.c's test_environment() clobbers HOME and PATH deliberately,
to prove neither survives into the exec'd environment, and restores both with
strdup'd copies. The first version used a fixed buffer and aborted the whole run
on a long PATH; the second forgot to restore HOME and broke every fixture
created after it. Both are why the save/restore is explicit.

------------------------------------------------------------- run from helper/

`make test` runs the binary from the helper directory, and t/fixtures/auth is
resolved relative to the working directory. Running ./build/vu-test from
elsewhere fails with "cannot open t/fixtures/auth" rather than silently skipping
the shared fixtures — a fixture set nobody loads is worse than no fixture set.

--------------------------------------------------- platform differences to know

Feature-test macros are set ONCE, in the Makefile, per platform:

  Linux    -D_GNU_SOURCE       -std=c99 defines __STRICT_ANSI__, and glibc then
                               hides mkdtemp, setenv, symlink and friends behind
                               a feature macro. Implicit declarations plus
                               -Werror is a broken build.
  Darwin   -D_DARWIN_C_SOURCE  defining _POSIX_C_SOURCE here RESTRICTS the
                               namespace instead of widening it: it hides flock,
                               setgroups and O_NOFOLLOW, and makes <libproc.h>
                               fail to compile.

So the portable-looking choice (_POSIX_C_SOURCE everywhere) swaps a Linux
failure for a macOS one. Do not add per-file #defines; the test files were
forgotten last time, which is exactly the failure mode a per-file convention
invites.

Diagnostics that only exist in GCC, and therefore only fail in CI:

  -Wformat-truncation   rejects snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, "%s/a", other) when
                        buf and other are the same size. Every path join in the
                        corpus goes through vu_path() (t/harness.h), which
                        cannot truncate, instead of checking fifteen return
                        values.
  -Wsign-conversion     stricter than clang's about ssize_t used as an index.

Symlinks:

  /var is a symlink on macOS, so a blanket O_NOFOLLOW walk cannot even create
  /var/run/vpn-up. The policy is leaf-only O_NOFOLLOW for directories we create,
  and resolve-then-verify (realpath, then check every component of the real
  path) for pinned paths. BSD returns ENOTDIR where Linux returns ELOOP for
  O_NOFOLLOW|O_DIRECTORY on a link; both are handled.

Sanitizers:

  make ubsan   works on both platforms; CI runs it on both
  make asan    Linux only. On macOS 26 the AddressSanitizer runtime spins
               forever inside its own initialiser — a stack sample shows it
               stuck in __asan::AsanInitFromRtl() under __malloc_init, before
               main() is reached. Do not read a macOS asan hang as a finding in
               this code.

---------------------------------------------------- synthetic ELF fixtures

t/test_closure.c builds ELF images byte by byte rather than testing against
whatever binary is installed. That is not only because there is no Linux
toolchain on the development machine: a synthesised image is the only way to get
a truncated header, a string table pointing outside the file, a big-endian ELF32
or a DT_RUNPATH of "$ORIGIN/../lib" on demand and deterministically.

One detail in the fixture is load-bearing. The PT_LOAD segment is given a
non-zero p_vaddr, so DT_STRTAB — which is a VIRTUAL ADDRESS, not a file offset —
does not coincide with its offset. A parser that confuses the two works on most
real binaries, where the first segment maps at 0 and the two happen to be equal,
and reads garbage on the rest. The fixture fails such a parser immediately.

`make test-elf-closure` rebuilds the corpus with -DVU_LIBRARY_CLOSURE_ELF=1 and
runs it, so the Linux-only library-closure branch is compiled and exercised here
rather than first meeting a compiler in CI. `make test` depends on it.

Forcing that macro on cannot weaken production: against a REAL macOS install the
ELF parse of a Mach-O binary reports "not an ELF binary" and the check still
refuses. Only the synthetic fixtures get past it.

------------------------------------------- fixture paths in failure messages

Every fixture message in this corpus prints the full path it was working on:

    CHECK(fd >= 0, "cannot create %s: %s", path, strerror(errno));

CodeQL flags that as cpp/system-data-exposure (CWE-497), because `path` is
derived from $HOME or $TMPDIR and the rule tracks environment data reaching an
output. It is a false positive here, and the reasoning is worth keeping so the
next alert does not have to be re-litigated:

  - The rule is about data reaching a REMOTE user - its own example sends the
    value over a socket. This is a test binary writing to its own stderr, read by
    whoever ran it, and they set $HOME.
  - The path is what makes a failure diagnosable. "cannot create: No such file or
    directory" does not say which fixture, and the /tmp-versus-$HOME behaviour
    documented above was diagnosed precisely by reading a path out of a failure
    message.

What matters is that the SHIPPED code does not have the same shape, and it does
not, by construction rather than by luck:

  - There is exactly one getenv in src/ - SUDO_UID, in vu_sudo_uid - and its
    value is never echoed. Both refusals are fixed strings ("SUDO_UID is not
    set", "SUDO_UID is not a plausible uid"), which is already this rule's own
    recommendation. It was written that way for an unrelated reason: telling a
    caller whether its value looked plausible is feedback it does not need.
  - Neither privileged binary performs any network I/O at all. No socket,
    connect, send or recv appears anywhere in src/. That is not incidental -
    two-phase exists so the privileged side never talks to the network - so
    there is no remote consumer for the rule to be about.

Everything the closure report prints (ownership, modes, resolved paths) is
obtainable by any user with ls -l and readlink -f, so it crosses no privilege
boundary even though root produced it.

If this alert recurs, the disposition is "used in tests", not a code change. And
it will recur: test_adversarial.c, test_exec.c, test_registry.c and test_state.c
all build fixture paths from the environment the same way.
------------------------------------------------------------- integration

t/test_integration.c performs a real execve into a real second process, using the
helper's own functions in the helper's own order. It needs no root and no VPN
gateway, which is what makes it a test rather than a procedure: the state root
and expected owner have been parameters since step 5 precisely so the privileged
sequence could be exercised unprivileged.

t/integration/fake-openconnect.c is the stand-in. It is a compiled C program and
not a script for a specific reason: the closure check parses the pinned binary's
ELF dynamic section, and a script is refused as "not an ELF binary" - a stand-in
that could not pass the real check would not be standing in for much. It reports
to STDOUT rather than to a file named by an environment variable, because the
helper hands its child a constructed environment with PATH and nothing else.

Two scripts, both opt-in:

  t/integration/run.sh               the real binaries, as root, end to end.
                                     VPN_UP_INTEGRATION=1, Linux only. Creates
                                     and removes /opt/vpn-up-integration and
                                     touches nothing else.
  t/integration/openconnect-probe.sh questions only the real OpenConnect can
                                     answer: which proxy schemes it supports
                                     (design 17.5) and whether it leaves an
                                     inherited locked descriptor alone (18).

Two things the integration test found about itself, both worth knowing before
writing another one:

  - The stand-in's path must be ABSOLUTE. The first version used the relative
    path the Makefile builds and the child died at execve with no explanation,
    because vu_harden_process does chdir("/") first. That is the design's
    absolute-path pin demonstrating itself.
  - SIGPIPE must be ignored while writing the cookie. Without that, a child that
    dies before reading kills the test runner with signal 13 and the only output
    is an exit code, which took a second run to diagnose. A harness whose failure
    mode is "the harness dies" cannot report anything.

---------------------------------------------------------- what is NOT tested yet

By design, because it needs something this corpus does not have:

  a real VPN gateway: two-phase against a live server, cookie lifetime
    (design 17.4), whether any protocol needs the client certificate at
    connect rather than only at auth (17.3)
  the macOS dyld closure: LC_LOAD_DYLIB, the shared cache, DYLD_*      (step 13)

The ACL case IS tested, on macOS, using chmod +a to grant write access to a file
whose mode bits are 0444 — mode bits say no, the ACL says yes, and the effective
probe has to notice. It is skipped with a note if the filesystem has no ACL
support. The Linux equivalent (setfacl) is not exercised yet.

Until step 13 exists the helper is not a finished boundary on macOS, and §11.7
keeps helper mode failing closed there — which t/test_closure.c now asserts as
behaviour rather than trusting the document.
